# Decided
- trueqbit asked whether the schema-level operations need listing;
  Claude: no - the enumeration (search, relate; then aggregate) already
  missed foreign_keys_count once, and every missing kind would read as a
  misfiling. trueqbit: rewrite around the single test, its own commit.
- The test: does the answer about an element depend on that element
  alone (classification, vocabulary: a trait, or a filter by one -
  index_filters.h), or on other elements of a collection, or the
  collection as a whole (schema-level: table_lookup.h, sync_order.h,
  foreign_keys.h as examples)?
- The placement test's arrow "not vocabulary." becomes "a schema-level
  algorithm, in dev/schema/algorithms/" (rule wording).

# Discussed
- Search by lookup type sits close to a filter followed by "pick the one
  match"; it is schema-level because the pick depends on the rest of the
  collection (exactly one match). Raised to trueqbit as a fine line; the
  test covers it.
- Replaced wording, 55999e7a and 67289968: "Algorithms that search,
  relate or aggregate nodes across a collection are schema-level
  algorithms: searching finds a node by a lookup type (table_lookup.h),
  relating sets one node against another (sync_order.h), aggregating
  combines an answer over all of them (foreign_keys.h) ... Filtering a
  collection by a trait each element answers alone is classification, and
  stays vocabulary: index_filters.h keeps the positions of the matching
  elements."
